<http://data.silknow.org/event/bde0610b-6eae-35f3-99f5-cebbd71288b4>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "'Mashru' is a satin weave cloth with a combination of a cotton weft and silk warp, the cotton weft being the lower layer in contact with the skin. The fabric was originally woven for Muslim men who were prohibited from wearing pure silk.  'Mashru' (meaning 'permitted' in Arabic) was woven all over India, though it survives today mainly in Gujarat."@en ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/c1c0a898-8717-3cb0-9875-53ac9ef0f757> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "'Mashru' is a satin weave cloth with a combination of a cotton weft and silk warp, the cotton weft being the lower layer in contact with the skin. The fabric was originally woven for Muslim men who were prohibited from wearing pure silk.  'Mashru' (meaning 'permitted' in Arabic) was woven all over India, though it survives today mainly in Gujarat."@en .

